DAY 26 – ENCAPSULATION

1. ENCAPSULATION

Encapsulation is an OOP concept used to bundle
data and methods together inside a class.

It also helps protect data and control access
to the data.


2. PUBLIC MEMBERS

Public attributes and methods can be accessed
directly from outside the class.


3. PROTECTED MEMBERS

Protected members are represented using a
single underscore (_).

They are intended to be accessed within the class
and its subclasses.


4. PRIVATE MEMBERS

Private members are represented using double
underscores (__).

They are used to restrict direct access from
outside the class.


5. GETTER METHODS

Getter methods are used to safely access the
values of private attributes.

They provide controlled access to private data.


6. SETTER METHODS

Setter methods are used to modify attribute values.

They can also apply validation when required
before changing the value.


7. ACCESS CONTROL

Encapsulation provides different levels of access
to class members:

Public → Direct access from outside the class.

Protected → Intended for use within the class
and its subclasses.

Private → Restricts direct access from outside
the class.


8. DATA PROTECTION

Encapsulation helps protect data by controlling
how class attributes are accessed and modified.

Getter and setter methods provide controlled
access to private attributes.


KEY TAKEAWAY

Encapsulation → Bundles data and methods together
inside a class and controls access to data.

Public → Directly accessible.

Protected (_) → Intended for class and subclasses.

Private (__) → Restricts direct outside access.

Getter → Safely accesses private attribute values.

Setter → Modifies attribute values with validation
when required.